<Brief
  brief={brief}
  standfirst="A quiet week for federal tax: no new legislation, guides or clarifications. The one material development was an enforcement action in the DIFC, and its lesson reaches well beyond it."
>
  <p>
    <strong>Review window: Monday 12 October to Sunday 18 October 2026.</strong>
  </p>

  <p>
    No federal tax instrument was published in the window. The Federal Tax
    Authority added nothing to its legislation, guides or public clarification
    registers, and the Ministry of Finance published no legislation. The
    week's one material development came from the Dubai Financial Services
    Authority, which on <strong>15 October 2026</strong> fined a
    DIFC-incorporated company <strong>USD 454,367</strong> for providing
    company service provider services — acting as formation agent and
    supplying registered office and business address services to 71 clients
    between August 2018 and February 2025 — without being registered as a
    Designated Non-Financial Business or Profession. The company's licensed
    activities were management consultancy and document clearing.
  </p>

  <p>
    The read-across is not confined to the DIFC: the mismatch between what a
    licence permits and what a business actually does is the same exposure
    that drives federal DNFBP registration, goAML obligations and, for a free
    zone entity, the integrity of a Qualifying Free Zone Person position.
    Separately, the ADGM Registration Authority opened a consultation on
    bringing business centres into licensed regulation.
  </p>

  <h2>Act now</h2>

  <section class="nt-brief-item" aria-labelledby="act-dnfbp">
    <h3 id="act-dnfbp">DNFBP registration — DFSA enforcement action of 15 October 2026</h3>
    <dl>
      <div>
        <dt>What changed</dt>
        <dd>
          No rule changed. The regulator enforced an existing prohibition and
          published the outcome. Article 71A(1) of the Regulatory Law 2004 bars
          a Designated Non-Financial Business or Profession from operating in or
          from the DIFC unless registered with the DFSA. The penalty was USD
          454,367.05, after a 30% settlement discount from USD 522,047.05, and
          includes a disgorgement component. The conduct was unlicensed company
          service provider activity — formation agency and registered office,
          business address, correspondence and administrative address services
          — carried on for seven years alongside a licence for management
          consultancy and document clearing.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Who it affects</dt>
        <dd>
          Any entity in or from the DIFC carrying on company service provider,
          accounting, audit, legal, trust, or real estate brokerage activity. By
          analogy, every UAE entity whose actual activity reaches the DNFBP
          definition federally or in another free zone, and every adviser who
          has set up or administers UAE structures for clients.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Action required</dt>
        <dd>
          Reconcile what the business actually does against what its trade
          licence permits, and test the actual activity against the DNFBP
          definition in the relevant jurisdiction — DFSA registration in the
          DIFC, ADGM registration in ADGM, Ministry of Economy and Tourism
          registration and goAML enrolment on the mainland and in commercial
          free zones. Where registered office, address, nominee or formation
          services are being supplied under a consultancy or clearing licence,
          treat that as a live exposure and take advice before the next client
          engagement rather than after an inspection. Where the activity is in
          scope, register before continuing to provide the service;
          registration is a precondition, not a remediation step.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Deadline</dt>
        <dd>
          <strong>Immediate.</strong> The prohibition is in force and carries
          no transition period. The seven-year conduct period in this case shows
          the regulator will look back, and that settlement reduces but does not
          avoid the penalty.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="nt-brief-item" aria-labelledby="act-strata">
