  <h2>Developments</h2>

  <h3>
    Dubai Financial Services Authority — penalty of USD 454,367 on a
    DIFC-incorporated company for breaching the DNFBP Prohibition (15 October 2026)
  </h3>

  <p>
    The DFSA imposed a penalty of USD 454,367.05, equivalent to AED
    1,668,662.99, on a company incorporated in the DIFC and licensed for
    management consultancy and document clearing services. The company was
    never authorised by the DFSA nor registered as a Designated Non-Financial
    Business or Profession, yet provided company service provider services in
    or from the DIFC: it acted as a formation agent for legal persons and
    provided registered office, business address, correspondence and
    administrative address services. It served 71 clients in this way between
    August 2018 and February 2025, and had also signed agreements with three
    free zones outside the DIFC to assist with company set-up and other company
    service provider services.
  </p>

  <p>
    The DFSA found a breach of Article 71A(1) of the Regulatory Law 2004 and
    reduced the penalty by 30% from USD 522,047.05 because the company agreed
    to settle; the figure includes disgorgement as well as a fine. The
    regulator's Managing Director, Enforcement stated that company service
    provider and other DNFBP services can carry higher financial crime risk,
    that registration is what allows the regulator to monitor those activities
    and confirm safeguards are in place, and that it has "zero tolerance for
    breaches that compromise the integrity of its anti-money laundering
    regime".
  </p>

  <p><strong>Impact</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>UAE mainland SMEs — Medium.</strong> No DFSA rule reaches a
      mainland entity, but the failure pattern does. A mainland or commercial
      free zone company that supplies registered office, formation, nominee or
      administrative address services under a general consultancy licence is in
      the same position in substance: the activity engages the federal DNFBP
      framework, which carries its own registration and goAML reporting duties
      and its own penalty regime. The practical test is not what the licence
      says but what the invoices describe.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Free zone entities and QFZP claimants — High where in the DIFC or
      ADGM, Medium elsewhere.</strong> For a DIFC or ADGM entity the prohibition
      applies directly and the registration gate is absolute. For a Qualifying
      Free Zone Person the point is sharper than compliance housekeeping:
      qualifying income depends on the activity actually carried on being a
      Qualifying Activity conducted with adequate substance, so an entity whose
      real activity sits outside its licensed activity has created a Corporate
      Tax problem at the same time as a licensing one. An entity relying on a
      0% position should not be able to be described more accurately by its
      enforcement file than by its licence.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cross-border service providers and DNFBPs — High.</strong> This
      is the direct target. A UK or EU firm that administers UAE entities,
      provides a registered address, or sub-contracts formation work into the
      UAE should assume the activity is in scope somewhere and identify where.
      Seven years of conduct and a disgorgement component mean the economics
      are not a cost of doing business; the fee income is recoverable by the
      regulator.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>On proportion.</strong> This is an enforcement outcome, not a
    legal change, and it should not be reported as a new obligation. Its value
    is evidential: it shows what the regulator treats as company service
    provider activity, that it will reconstruct a multi-year conduct period,
    and that it will strip out the revenue earned. Any alert presenting it as a
    change to the DNFBP regime is wrong. Any alert presenting it as a
    DIFC-only matter is reading it too narrowly.
  </p>

  <p class="nt-brief__sources">
    Sources:
    <a href="https://www.dfsa.ae/news/hayman-woodward-global-mobility-services-difc-ltd" rel="noopener">DFSA — financial penalty for breaching the DNFBP Prohibition, 15 October 2026</a>
    ·
    <a href="https://www.dfsa.ae/news" rel="noopener">DFSA — news register</a>
  </p>

  <h3>
    Abu Dhabi Global Market — Registration Authority Consultation Paper No. 7 of
    2026, proposed regulatory framework for business centres (15 October 2026,
    closes 6 November 2026)
  </h3>

  <p>
    The ADGM Registration Authority has proposed amendments to the Commercial
    Licensing Regulations and their subordinate rules — the Controlled
    Activities Rules, the Conditions of Licence and Branch Registration Rules,
    and the Fees Rules — to bring business centres into regulated activity.
    The proposals would classify the provision of business centre services as
    a controlled activity, and impose public liability insurance, clear
    pricing disclosure, limits on the term of client agreements, notification
    duties, wind-down and contingency planning, minimum regulatory capital, a
    set of regulatory principles, and a prohibition on accepting or
    distributing cash above a prescribed threshold. The Authority states the
    aim as protecting business centre tenants and supporting continuity of
    their premises and registered office arrangements. Comments close on 6
    November 2026. The paper is a consultation and creates no present
    obligation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Impact</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>UAE mainland SMEs — Low.</strong> The proposals reach ADGM only.
      The signal worth noting is directional: regulators are treating
      registered office and address provision as an activity warranting
      supervision, which is the same instinct visible in the DFSA action above.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Free zone entities and QFZP claimants — Medium where in
      ADGM.</strong> A large share of smaller ADGM entities hold their
      registered office through a business centre. Minimum capital, insurance
      and wind-down requirements on operators will show up as price and, for
      weaker operators, as exit — and a registered office is where statutory
      notices and tax correspondence land. An entity whose registered office
      provider fails has a continuity problem, not an administrative one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cross-border service providers and DNFBPs — Medium.</strong>
      Company service providers that operate business centres as an adjunct to
      corporate services would face licensing as a controlled activity, capital
      and insurance requirements, and a cash-handling prohibition. Firms with
      ADGM clients that intend to comment had under three weeks at the date of
      this brief.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="nt-brief__sources">
    Sources:
    <a href="https://www.adgm.com/media/announcements/adgm-ra-publishes-consultation-paper-on-enhanced-business-centre-framework" rel="noopener">ADGM — RA publishes consultation paper on enhanced business centre framework, 15 October 2026</a>
    ·
    <a href="https://www.adgm.com/legal-framework/public-consultations" rel="noopener">ADGM — public consultations register</a>
  </p>

  <h3>Nothing further of substance in the window</h3>

  <p>
    The Federal Tax Authority published no legislation between 12 and 18
    October 2026; the most recent entry on its consolidated register remains
    FTA Decision No. 17 of 2026, published 28 September 2026. No Cabinet
    Decision, Ministerial Decision or FTA Decision on Corporate Tax, VAT or
    Excise Tax was added. No guide or public clarification was issued on
    either the Corporate Tax or the VAT register; the most recent remain
    TAXP010 and VATP047, both of 2 October 2026. The announcements register
    carried no new announcement and remains the source of the 28 October 2026
    VAT filing deadline below. The Authority's most recent news item remains
    the Top-up Tax guide release of 7 to 8 October 2026.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Ministry of Finance published two news items, both on 18 October 2026,
    on the conclusion of UAE participation in the IMF and World Bank Group
    annual meetings in Bangkok and on an agreement to host the 2029 annual
    meetings in Abu Dhabi; neither has a compliance consequence. The Ministry
    of Economy and Tourism issued no new anti-money-laundering circular; the
    latest remains Circular No. 2 of 2026. No Financial Action Task Force
    plenary outcome was published in the window. The Central Bank of the UAE
    legislation register carried nothing in the window. No Economic Substance,
    Country-by-Country Reporting, transfer pricing, FATCA or CRS instrument
    was published by any authority.
  </p>

